# PART 3 — PROJECT MATERIAL ALLOCATION AND HISTORY

The Site Supervisor must have a complete view of materials allocated to every project under their supervision.

This must include materials assigned at the beginning of the project and additional materials requested during execution.

Create or improve the project Materials workspace.

## A. Initial project material allocation

When a project becomes active, the supervisor should be able to see the initial materials allocated to the project from its approved BOQ or authorised material allocation.

Display:

- Material name.
- Material specification.
- Approved quantity.
- Quantity initially issued.
- Date issued.
- Issuing department.
- Remaining available quantity.

Do not assume that a BOQ estimate is an actual stock issue.

Clearly distinguish projected quantities from physically issued quantities.

## B. Additional material requests

Show every subsequent material request submitted during the project.

Display:

- Request reference.
- Request date.
- Material.
- Quantity requested.
- Quantity approved.
- Quantity issued.
- Approval status.
- Approving officer.
- Issue date.

## C. Project material summary

For every project, provide:

- Initial allocation.
- Additional approved allocations.
- Total quantities received by the project.
- Quantities used.
- Quantities wasted or damaged.
- Quantities returned.
- Current project-held balance.

Use a clear material movement history.

Calculate:

Project Material Balance = Total Project Issues + Other Valid Receipts - Recorded Usage - Recorded Waste - Returns to Store.

Ensure each movement is recorded exactly once.

Project material usage must not be confused with Store inventory deduction.

An approved issue deducts from Store and increases project-held stock.

Subsequent site consumption reduces project-held stock.

A return to Store reduces project-held stock and increases available Store stock through a corresponding transaction.

Do not allow the same material to be deducted twice.

## D. Management visibility

Management and Procurement should be able to compare:

- Approved BOQ quantities.
- Actual Store issues.
- Project consumption.
- Waste.
- Remaining project stock.
- Production achieved.

Flag abnormal material consumption for investigation.

